Credential Access – NTDS.dit Investigation Report
1. Investigation Approach

As the case was related to NTDS.dit, the investigation was initiated by searching the logs using the keyword ntds.dit to identify events associated with access to the Active Directory database.

2. Initial Finding

During the investigation, an event was identified where the filtering component reported:

“Filtering has permitted to bind to local port.”

The event was associated with the process dfsrs.exe and an unregistered/local port 55442.

The event was reviewed to determine whether the network activity was associated with suspicious access to the NTDS.dit database.

3. Process Investigation

Further investigation was performed on the associated cmd.exe process.

The SHA/hash of the identified cmd.exe was investigated and found to correspond to a file associated with Google Android Compatibility Test Suite (CTS) testing.

The identified archive was:

android-cts-7.1_r6-linux_x86-arm.zip

The investigation indicated that this archive contains/creates a cmd.exe file, explaining the presence of the executable observed in the event.

4. Findings

The investigation established the following:

The case was investigated using the keyword ntds.dit.
An event involving binding to local port 55442 was identified.
The associated process was dfsrs.exe.
The cmd.exe hash was investigated.
The hash was associated with a cmd.exe file used in Google Android CTS testing.
The related archive was identified as android-cts-7.1_r6-linux_x86-arm.zip.
Based on the available evidence, the identified cmd.exe appears to have a legitimate testing-related origin rather than being sufficient by itself to establish malicious NTDS.dit credential access.
5. Conclusion

The investigation identified activity associated with the NTDS.dit credential-access case, including local port binding and execution involving cmd.exe. Hash analysis provided additional context indicating that the identified cmd.exe was associated with the Google Android CTS testing package.

Therefore, the observed cmd.exe activity should be considered in the context of the identified testing package, and additional evidence would be required to confirm unauthorized NTDS.dit access or credential dumping.
